--TEST--
a request tree that deferred work keeps running once its answer is complete, and the answer is complete: nothing the deferred callable writes can reach the response the caller already holds — `rule:concurrency/after-response-outlives-the-connection`, `rule:testing/in-process-request`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `task-after-response-runs` asks *when* a deferred callable runs, on a host
// that has no response for anything to be after. This case asks the other
// half, which only a request can be asked: § 6's "the callable runs after the
// response is fully written", read from the side of the caller holding that
// response.
//
// The shape is *a bound asserted on both sides*, over one request: the last
// bytes the answer carries and the first bytes it does not are named together.
// A queue that ran its callables inline — before the frame returned — would put
// `deferred` inside the body below, and a response that stayed writable after
// the connection was answered would put it there too. Both are what § 6
// forbids, and either one takes this case off its expectation.
//
// **The branch is the request, and the `catch` is how a case tells the two
// sides apart** — an in-process request may not be made from inside one, which
// `an-in-process-request-may-not-be-made-from-inside-one` pins in full.
try {
    var $answered = Core\Test::request(Core\Http\Method::Get, "/orders/7");
    // The caller's whole view of the request: a status and the bytes. Both are
    // read after the child's tree, deferred work and all, has been joined, so
    // this is not a race the case is winning by being quick.
    echo "status=", $answered->status(), "\n";
    echo "body=", $answered->body(), "\n";

    // The parent's own tree keeps its own queue — § 6's trigger on a host with
    // no response is the scheduling frame returning — so this runs last, after
    // the two lines above and after every statement of this file. Two trees,
    // two queues, and neither one's deferred work reached the other's output.
    Core\Task::afterResponse(fn (): void => {
        echo "the parent's own tree, after its last statement\n";
    });
} catch (RuntimeError $refused) {
    // The child. It registers deferred work and then finishes answering; § 6
    // keeps `Core\Request` readable inside the callable because the tree is
    // still the same tree, and keeps `Core\Response` out of it because the
    // response is not.
    Core\Task::afterResponse(fn (): void => {
        echo " deferred=", Core\Request::path();
    });
    echo "answered";
}
--EXPECT--
status=200
body=answered
the parent's own tree, after its last statement
